# IFD is fine

[Import From Derivation](https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.34/language/import-from-derivation) means Nix evaluation reads a file that a build has to produce first.

```nix
let
  schemaJson = pkgs.runCommand "schema" {} ''
    ${pkgs.codegen}/bin/codegen ${./input.toml} > $out
  '';
  schema = builtins.fromJSON (builtins.readFile schemaJson);
in
  buildPackage { inherit schema; }
```

Evaluation cannot get past `schema` until `schemaJson` is built. The community calls this an anti-pattern, and [`nix flake check`](https://github.com/NixOS/nix/pull/5253) and Hydra reject it.

## The slowness is CppNix, not the idea

CppNix evaluates one thing at a time. When it hits an IFD it stops, waits for the build, and resumes, so everything else waits too. [As jade.fyi puts it](https://jade.fyi/blog/nix-evaluation-blocking/), the evaluator "cannot queue all the derivations that are reachable, rather than stopping and building each in order."

[Snix](https://snix.dev/blog/announcing-snix/), a Rust reimplementation, has no such limit. A thunk that needs a build yields, other work continues, and the thunk resumes when the build lands. The [Tvix design](https://tvl.fyi/blog/rewriting-nix) treats IFD as "a first-class citizen without significant performance cost."

## Banning it has a price

Without IFD, everything the build needs must be readable from pure Nix. So tools re-implement other tools: [crane](https://github.com/ipetkov/crane) re-implements part of cargo's resolver, git projects commit precomputed JSON, and generated code gets checked in by hand. A ban on one evaluator's weakness became a ceiling on what Nix can compose.

## Still, a static graph is better

IFD is a choice made at runtime: what to build next depends on a result. [Build Systems a la Carte](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/uploads/prod/2018/03/build-systems.pdf) calls that monadic, and shows it costs you minimal rebuilds unless the system remembers its previous graph.

When a tool can print its whole graph up front, you get all of that for free. [`cargo build --unit-graph`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/unstable.html#unit-graph) does exactly that. Each unit's key is FNV-1a over its command and the keys of the units it reads, so the numbers are mine and not the sha256 of a real spec. A changed input changes the key of every action downstream and nothing else.

::exhibit{id="graph" place="wide"}

[Buck2](https://buck2.build/docs/rule_authors/dynamic_dependencies/) keeps a fenced escape hatch, `dynamic_output`, for edges that only a scanner can find, like OCaml imports. Use the dynamic form there and the static graph everywhere else.

## The ban was a workaround

"IFD is an anti-pattern" was a polite way to say the reference evaluator cannot handle it yet. The phrasing outlived the constraint.
